<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.joel.keymap</string>

  <!-- ¡ (the key right of ?, HID usage 0x2E, "equal" in US names) becomes
       F12 at the HID layer, so it never types a character in any app. Herdr
       binds F12 as its prefix key (herdr/config.toml [keys]). A printable
       prefix would both open the prefix and type the glyph; F12 has no glyph,
       and the physical F12 sends volume-up, not F12, while
       com.apple.keyboard.fnState is off — so nothing collides.

       F12 (usage 0x45), not F13 (0x68). F13 was tried first and the prefix
       sometimes failed to open. Why is not known: Herdr accepts
       `prefix = "f13"`, and its binary carries F13's ESC [25~ alongside the
       sequences it does act on. So this rests on the measurement and states
       no mechanism. F12 has been reliable.

       What F12 costs is that it is a real key. Nothing collides while
       com.apple.keyboard.fnState is off, because the physical F12 then sends
       volume-up rather than F12 — but turn fnState on, or press fn+F12, and
       that key opens the prefix. F13 has no physical key at all and carries
       no such caveat, which is why it is worth returning to if the F13 fault
       is ever understood.

       hidutil mappings are lost at logout and on keyboard reconnect, so this
       runs at login. Dst is the 64-bit HID usage: 0x700000000 | usage. -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/bin/hidutil</string>
    <string>property</string>
    <string>--set</string>
    <string>{"UserKeyMapping":[{"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingSrc":0x70000002E,"HIDKeyboardModifierMappingDst":0x700000045}]}</string>
  </array>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
